WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:02.456 --> 00:06.286 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference May 4,

00:06.286 --> 00:07.886 align:middle
2022 CHAIR POWELL.

00:07.886 --> 00:08.246 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:08.736 --> 00:10.696 align:middle
It's nice to see
everyone in person

00:10.696 --> 00:12.496 align:middle
for the first time
in a couple years.

00:12.496 --> 00:14.666 align:middle
Before I go into the
details of today's meeting,

00:14.716 --> 00:17.776 align:middle
I'd like to take this
opportunity to speak directly

00:17.776 --> 00:18.486 align:middle
to the American people.

00:18.546 --> 00:19.606 align:middle
Inflation is much too high,

00:20.036 --> 00:21.846 align:middle
and we understand the
hardship it is causing,

00:22.566 --> 00:24.946 align:middle
and we're moving expeditiously
to bring it back down.

00:26.246 --> 00:28.166 align:middle
We have both the tools
we need and the resolve

00:28.166 --> 00:30.696 align:middle
that it will take to restore
price stability on behalf

00:30.696 --> 00:32.476 align:middle
of American families
and businesses.

00:32.976 --> 00:35.976 align:middle
The economy and the country
have been through a lot

00:35.976 --> 00:38.336 align:middle
over the past two years
and have proved resilient.

00:38.546 --> 00:41.946 align:middle
It is essential that we bring
inflation down if we are

00:41.946 --> 00:43.466 align:middle
to have a sustained period

00:43.466 --> 00:45.936 align:middle
of strong labor market
conditions that benefit all.

00:46.396 --> 00:50.326 align:middle
From the standpoint of
our Congressional mandate

00:50.326 --> 00:52.646 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and price stability,

00:52.786 --> 00:54.566 align:middle
the current picture
is plain to see:

00:55.476 --> 00:56.976 align:middle
The labor market
is extremely tight,

00:57.366 --> 00:58.846 align:middle
and inflation is much too high.

01:00.286 --> 01:03.936 align:middle
Against this backdrop, today the
FOMC raised its policy interest

01:03.936 --> 01:06.856 align:middle
rate by ½ percentage
point and anticipates

01:06.856 --> 01:08.716 align:middle
that ongoing increases
in the target rate

01:08.716 --> 01:10.566 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate will be appropriate.

01:11.076 --> 01:14.006 align:middle
In addition, we are
beginning the process

01:14.006 --> 01:16.786 align:middle
of significantly reducing the
size of our balance sheet.

01:17.636 --> 01:20.546 align:middle
I'll have more to say about
today's monetary policy actions

01:20.636 --> 01:22.796 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

01:24.836 --> 01:28.036 align:middle
After expanding at a robust
5½ percent pace last year,

01:28.496 --> 01:31.516 align:middle
overall economic activity edged
down in the first quarter.

01:32.566 --> 01:34.566 align:middle
Underlying momentum
remains strong, however,

01:34.796 --> 01:38.376 align:middle
as the decline largely
reflected swings in inventories

01:38.376 --> 01:42.026 align:middle
and net exports-two volatile
categories whose movements last

01:42.026 --> 01:45.196 align:middle
quarter likely carry little
signal for future growth.

01:46.386 --> 01:47.516 align:middle
Indeed, household spending

01:47.516 --> 01:50.526 align:middle
and business fixed investment
continued to expand briskly.

01:51.116 --> 01:54.276 align:middle
The labor market has
continued to strengthen

01:54.276 --> 01:55.456 align:middle
and is extremely tight.

01:56.236 --> 01:58.626 align:middle
Over the first three months
of the year, employment rose

01:58.626 --> 02:00.936 align:middle
by nearly 1.7 million jobs.

02:01.626 --> 02:04.316 align:middle
In March, the unemployment
rate hit a post-pandemic,

02:04.316 --> 02:07.276 align:middle
and near-five-decade,
low of 3.6 percent.

02:08.866 --> 02:11.366 align:middle
Improvements in labor market
conditions have been widespread,

02:11.966 --> 02:13.546 align:middle
including for workers
at the lower end

02:13.546 --> 02:15.526 align:middle
of the wage distribution as well

02:15.526 --> 02:17.456 align:middle
as for African Americans
and Hispanics.

02:18.856 --> 02:20.756 align:middle
Labor demand is very
strong, and,

02:20.756 --> 02:23.366 align:middle
while labor force participation
has increased somewhat,

02:23.966 --> 02:25.596 align:middle
labor supply remains subdued.

02:27.036 --> 02:28.906 align:middle
Employers are having
difficulties filling job

02:28.906 --> 02:30.676 align:middle
openings, and wages are rising

02:30.676 --> 02:32.666 align:middle
at the fastest pace
in many years.

02:34.096 --> 02:37.076 align:middle
Inflation remains well above our
longer-run goal of 2 percent.

02:38.726 --> 02:40.446 align:middle
Over the 12 months
ending in March,

02:40.476 --> 02:43.166 align:middle
total PCE prices
rose 6.6 percent;

02:44.146 --> 02:46.266 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

02:46.266 --> 02:48.846 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
5.2 percent.

02:50.106 --> 02:52.266 align:middle
Aggregate demand is
strong, and bottlenecks

02:52.266 --> 02:54.756 align:middle
and supply constraints are
limiting how quickly production

02:54.756 --> 02:55.406 align:middle
can respond.

02:56.456 --> 02:59.056 align:middle
Disruptions to supply have been
larger, and longer lasting,

02:59.056 --> 03:02.526 align:middle
than anticipated, and
price pressures have spread

03:02.526 --> 03:05.076 align:middle
to a broader range of
goods and services.

03:05.586 --> 03:09.286 align:middle
The surge in prices of crude
oil and other commodities

03:09.286 --> 03:10.976 align:middle
that resulted from
Russia's invasion

03:10.976 --> 03:13.806 align:middle
of Ukraine is creating
additional upward pressure

03:14.136 --> 03:14.796 align:middle
on inflation.

03:15.766 --> 03:18.156 align:middle
And COVID-related
lockdowns in China are likely

03:18.156 --> 03:21.386 align:middle
to further exacerbate supply
chain disruptions as well.

03:23.536 --> 03:26.436 align:middle
Russia's invasion of Ukraine
is causing tremendous loss

03:26.436 --> 03:28.656 align:middle
and hardship, and our
thoughts and sympathies are

03:28.656 --> 03:29.916 align:middle
with the people of Ukraine.

03:31.016 --> 03:32.816 align:middle
Our job is to consider
the implications

03:32.816 --> 03:35.346 align:middle
for the U.S. economy, which
remain highly uncertain.

03:36.566 --> 03:38.976 align:middle
In addition to the effects
on inflation, the invasion

03:38.976 --> 03:40.336 align:middle
and related events are likely

03:40.336 --> 03:42.426 align:middle
to restrain economic
activity abroad

03:43.056 --> 03:46.506 align:middle
and further disrupt supply
chains, creating spillovers

03:46.506 --> 03:48.946 align:middle
to the U.S. economy through
trade and other channels.

03:49.476 --> 03:53.446 align:middle
The Fed's monetary
policy actions are guided

03:53.446 --> 03:55.586 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

03:55.696 --> 03:57.646 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

03:59.306 --> 04:01.196 align:middle
My colleagues and
I are acutely aware

04:01.196 --> 04:03.456 align:middle
that high inflation imposes
significant hardship,

04:04.096 --> 04:06.706 align:middle
especially on those least
able to meet the higher costs

04:06.706 --> 04:09.906 align:middle
of essentials like food,
housing, and transportation.

04:11.276 --> 04:13.896 align:middle
We are highly attentive to the
risks that high inflation poses

04:13.896 --> 04:16.976 align:middle
to both sides of our mandate,
and we are strongly committed

04:16.976 --> 04:18.306 align:middle
to restoring price stability.

04:18.776 --> 04:21.096 align:middle
Against the backdrop

04:21.096 --> 04:23.186 align:middle
of the rapidly evolving
economic environment,

04:23.646 --> 04:26.346 align:middle
our policy has been adapting,
and it will continue to do so.

04:27.356 --> 04:28.396 align:middle
At today's meeting,

04:28.596 --> 04:32.466 align:middle
the Committee raised the target
range for the federal funds rate

04:32.976 --> 04:35.706 align:middle
by ½ percentage point and
stated that it "anticipates

04:35.706 --> 04:37.016 align:middle
that ongoing increases

04:37.076 --> 04:38.726 align:middle
in the target range
will be appropriate."

04:39.816 --> 04:42.406 align:middle
We also decided to begin the
process of reducing the size

04:42.406 --> 04:45.736 align:middle
of our balance sheet, which
will play an important role

04:45.736 --> 04:47.786 align:middle
in firming the stance
of monetary policy.

04:48.356 --> 04:52.386 align:middle
We are on a path to move our
policy rate expeditiously

04:52.386 --> 04:53.636 align:middle
to more normal levels.

04:54.716 --> 04:57.136 align:middle
Assuming that economic and
financial conditions evolve

04:57.136 --> 05:00.556 align:middle
in line with expectations,
there is a broad sense

05:00.556 --> 05:04.096 align:middle
on the Committee that additional
50-basis-point increases should

05:04.096 --> 05:06.166 align:middle
be on the table at the
next couple of meetings.

05:06.856 --> 05:10.686 align:middle
We will make our decisions
meeting by meeting as we learn

05:10.686 --> 05:12.666 align:middle
from incoming data and
the evolving outlook

05:12.666 --> 05:13.316 align:middle
for the economy.

05:14.626 --> 05:16.596 align:middle
And we will continue to
communicate our thinking

05:16.596 --> 05:17.916 align:middle
as clearly as possible.

05:19.256 --> 05:21.686 align:middle
Our overarching focus
is using our tools

05:21.686 --> 05:24.556 align:middle
to bring inflation back
down to our 2 percent goal.

05:27.186 --> 05:28.366 align:middle
With regard to our
balance sheet,

05:28.486 --> 05:30.346 align:middle
we also issued our
specific plans

05:30.346 --> 05:32.106 align:middle
for reducing our
securities holdings.

05:33.196 --> 05:35.396 align:middle
Consistent with the
principles we issued in January,

05:35.686 --> 05:37.616 align:middle
we intend to significantly
reduce the size

05:37.616 --> 05:39.836 align:middle
of our balance sheet over
time in a predictable manner

05:40.596 --> 05:41.936 align:middle
by allowing the principal
payments

05:41.936 --> 05:44.466 align:middle
from our securities holdings
to roll off the balance sheet,

05:45.136 --> 05:46.496 align:middle
up to monthly cap amounts.

05:47.526 --> 05:50.716 align:middle
For Treasury securities, the cap
will be $30 billion per month

05:50.716 --> 05:53.076 align:middle
for three months and
will then increase

05:53.076 --> 05:54.806 align:middle
to $60 billion per month.

05:56.046 --> 05:58.086 align:middle
The decline in holdings
of Treasury securities

05:58.086 --> 06:00.526 align:middle
under this monthly cap will
include Treasury coupon

06:00.526 --> 06:03.936 align:middle
securities and, to the extent
that coupon securities are less

06:03.966 --> 06:05.946 align:middle
than the monthly
cap, Treasury bills.

06:06.916 --> 06:09.076 align:middle
For agency mortgage-backed
securities,

06:09.076 --> 06:12.896 align:middle
the cap will be $17.5 billion
per month for three months

06:12.896 --> 06:15.306 align:middle
and will then increase
to $35 billion per month.

06:16.246 --> 06:18.846 align:middle
At the current level of
mortgage rates, the actual pace

06:18.846 --> 06:21.126 align:middle
of agency MBS runoff
would likely be less

06:21.176 --> 06:22.336 align:middle
than this monthly cap amount.

06:22.826 --> 06:26.896 align:middle
Our balance sheet decisions are
guided by our maximum-employment

06:26.896 --> 06:28.076 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

06:28.306 --> 06:31.296 align:middle
And, in that regard, we will
be prepared to adjust any

06:31.296 --> 06:33.636 align:middle
of the details of our
approach in light of economic

06:33.636 --> 06:34.936 align:middle
and financial developments.

06:37.256 --> 06:38.826 align:middle
Making appropriate
monetary policy

06:38.826 --> 06:41.046 align:middle
in this uncertain environment
requires a recognition

06:41.046 --> 06:43.496 align:middle
that the economy often
evolves in unexpected ways.

06:44.016 --> 06:46.306 align:middle
Inflation has obviously
surprised to the upside

06:46.306 --> 06:49.526 align:middle
over the past year, and further
surprises could be in store.

06:51.306 --> 06:53.526 align:middle
We therefore will need to
be nimble in responding

06:53.526 --> 06:55.486 align:middle
to incoming data and
the evolving outlook.

06:56.606 --> 06:58.786 align:middle
And we will strive to
avoid adding uncertainty

06:58.786 --> 07:01.176 align:middle
to what is already an
extraordinarily challenging

07:01.176 --> 07:02.046 align:middle
and uncertain time.

07:03.206 --> 07:06.526 align:middle
We are highly attentive
to inflation risks.

07:07.796 --> 07:10.106 align:middle
The Committee is determined
to take the measures necessary

07:10.106 --> 07:11.366 align:middle
to restore price stability.

07:12.526 --> 07:15.276 align:middle
The American economy is very
strong and well positioned

07:15.496 --> 07:17.326 align:middle
to handle tighter
monetary policy.

07:17.926 --> 07:22.076 align:middle
To conclude: We understand that
our actions affect communities,

07:22.076 --> 07:23.946 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

07:24.996 --> 07:27.416 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

07:28.656 --> 07:30.226 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

07:30.336 --> 07:31.806 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

07:31.806 --> 07:32.906 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

07:34.126 --> 07:36.376 align:middle
Thank you, and I look
forward to your questions.

07:39.976 --> 07:41.866 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

07:43.756 --> 07:47.786 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, the
Wall Street Journal.

07:49.026 --> 07:51.796 align:middle
Chair Powell, the
unemployment rate at 3.6 percent

07:51.796 --> 07:53.606 align:middle
in March is now essentially
at the level

07:54.046 --> 07:56.556 align:middle
that the Committee had
expected would prevail

07:56.556 --> 07:59.376 align:middle
over the next three years
and at the bottom end

07:59.376 --> 08:01.786 align:middle
of FOMC participants'
projections

08:02.166 --> 08:04.746 align:middle
for the longer-run
rate that you submitted

08:04.746 --> 08:06.176 align:middle
in the projections
at the last meeting.

08:06.806 --> 08:09.056 align:middle
How has your outlook
for further declines

08:09.056 --> 08:11.386 align:middle
in the unemployment rate
changed since March?

08:11.876 --> 08:15.156 align:middle
What does this imply for
your inflation forecast?

08:15.616 --> 08:19.816 align:middle
And how has your level of
confidence changed with regard

08:19.816 --> 08:22.346 align:middle
to the feasibility
of slowing hiring

08:22.386 --> 08:24.866 align:middle
without pushing the
economy into recession?

08:25.316 --> 08:27.706 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

08:27.706 --> 08:28.526 align:middle
Thank you.

08:28.756 --> 08:32.576 align:middle
So you're right-3.6
percent unemployment is just

08:32.796 --> 08:34.736 align:middle
about as low as it's
been in 50 years.

08:35.416 --> 08:38.396 align:middle
And I would say that I expect,

08:38.396 --> 08:40.156 align:middle
and Committee members
generally expect,

08:40.156 --> 08:42.466 align:middle
that we'll get some
additional participation.

08:42.466 --> 08:44.456 align:middle
So people will be coming
back into the labor force.

08:44.456 --> 08:46.606 align:middle
We've seen that particularly
among prime-age people.

08:47.306 --> 08:48.226 align:middle
And that will, of course,

08:48.226 --> 08:50.986 align:middle
tend to hold the unemployment
rate up a little bit.

08:51.646 --> 08:55.756 align:middle
I would also expect, though,
that job creation will slow.

08:56.046 --> 08:57.366 align:middle
Job creation has been, you know,

08:57.366 --> 09:00.626 align:middle
more than half a million per
month in recent months-very,

09:00.626 --> 09:03.766 align:middle
very strong, particularly for
this stage of the economy.

09:03.946 --> 09:07.306 align:middle
And so we think-with fiscal
policy less supportive,

09:07.306 --> 09:10.046 align:middle
with monetary policy
less supportive,

09:10.126 --> 09:12.306 align:middle
we think that job
creation will slow as well.

09:12.486 --> 09:14.556 align:middle
So it is certainly possible

09:14.556 --> 09:16.426 align:middle
that the unemployment
would go down further.

09:17.126 --> 09:22.026 align:middle
But so I would expect those
to be relatively limited

09:22.386 --> 09:24.116 align:middle
because of the additional supply

09:24.116 --> 09:28.686 align:middle
and also just the
slowing in job creation.

09:29.296 --> 09:31.426 align:middle
Implications for
inflation-really,

09:31.426 --> 09:35.316 align:middle
the wages matter a fair amount
for companies, particularly

09:35.316 --> 09:36.586 align:middle
in the service sector.

09:36.966 --> 09:37.906 align:middle
Wages are running high,

09:37.906 --> 09:40.716 align:middle
the highest they've
run in quite some time.

09:40.716 --> 09:45.316 align:middle
And they are one good example,
or good illustration, really,

09:45.316 --> 09:47.166 align:middle
of how tight the labor
market really is,

09:47.206 --> 09:48.716 align:middle
the fact that wages are running

09:48.716 --> 09:50.626 align:middle
at the highest level
in many decades.

09:51.146 --> 09:53.086 align:middle
And that's because of an
imbalance between supply

09:53.086 --> 09:54.206 align:middle
and demand in the labor market.

09:54.296 --> 09:56.986 align:middle
So we think through our
policies-through further healing

09:56.986 --> 10:00.166 align:middle
in the labor market,
higher rates, for example,

10:00.166 --> 10:01.886 align:middle
of vacancy filling
and things like that,

10:02.336 --> 10:04.556 align:middle
and more people coming
back in-we'd like to think

10:04.556 --> 10:07.396 align:middle
that supply and demand will
come back into balance and that,

10:07.396 --> 10:11.636 align:middle
therefore, wage inflation will
moderate to still high levels

10:11.636 --> 10:14.056 align:middle
of wage increases, but ones
that are more consistent

10:14.056 --> 10:15.246 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation.

10:16.486 --> 10:18.256 align:middle
That's our expectation.

10:18.416 --> 10:19.616 align:middle
Your third question was?

10:19.616 --> 10:20.456 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

10:20.456 --> 10:22.616 align:middle
Your level of confidence
that you can slow hiring

10:23.006 --> 10:24.976 align:middle
without pushing the
economy into a downturn.

10:24.976 --> 10:25.726 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:25.776 --> 10:32.366 align:middle
So I guess I would say it
this way: There's a path.

10:33.136 --> 10:38.646 align:middle
There's a path by which we would
be able to have demand moderate

10:38.926 --> 10:43.116 align:middle
in the labor market and
therefore have vacancies come

10:43.116 --> 10:46.556 align:middle
down without unemployment
going up, because vacancies are

10:46.556 --> 10:48.266 align:middle
at such an extraordinarily
high level.

10:48.266 --> 10:52.366 align:middle
There're 1.9 vacancies for
every unemployed person,

10:52.366 --> 10:56.036 align:middle
11.5 million vacancies, 6
million unemployed people.

10:56.116 --> 11:00.196 align:middle
So we haven't been in
that place on the vacancy,

11:00.336 --> 11:02.926 align:middle
sort of the vacancy/unemployed
curve, the Beveridge curve.

11:02.926 --> 11:05.046 align:middle
We haven't been at
that sort of level

11:05.156 --> 11:07.696 align:middle
of a ratio in the modern era.

11:08.106 --> 11:12.136 align:middle
So, in principle, it seems as
though by moderating demand,

11:12.786 --> 11:16.756 align:middle
we could see vacancies
come down and,

11:16.756 --> 11:19.706 align:middle
as a result-and they could come
down fairly significantly and,

11:19.706 --> 11:22.336 align:middle
I think, put supply and demand

11:22.336 --> 11:24.106 align:middle
at least closer together
than they are.

11:24.696 --> 11:29.466 align:middle
And that would give us a
chance to get inflation down,

11:29.496 --> 11:32.896 align:middle
get wages down, and then get
inflation down without having

11:32.896 --> 11:35.116 align:middle
to slow the economy
and have a recession

11:35.116 --> 11:37.226 align:middle
and have unemployment
rise materially.

11:37.426 --> 11:38.426 align:middle
So there's a path to that.

11:38.806 --> 11:41.126 align:middle
Now, I would say, I think
we have a good chance

11:41.796 --> 11:45.736 align:middle
to have a soft or softish
landing or outcome, if you will.

11:45.736 --> 11:47.286 align:middle
And I'll give you a
couple of reasons for that.

11:47.866 --> 11:49.956 align:middle
One is, households
and businesses are

11:49.956 --> 11:51.296 align:middle
in very strong financial shape.

11:51.426 --> 11:54.416 align:middle
You're looking at, you
know, excess savings

11:54.416 --> 11:56.596 align:middle
on balance sheets-excess
in the sense

11:56.596 --> 11:58.976 align:middle
that they're substantially
larger than the prior trend.

11:59.566 --> 12:01.256 align:middle
Businesses are in
good financial shape.

12:01.456 --> 12:04.226 align:middle
The labor market is, as I
mentioned, very, very strong.

12:04.796 --> 12:09.876 align:middle
And so it doesn't seem to be
anywhere close to a downturn.

12:10.296 --> 12:13.626 align:middle
Therefore, the economy is
strong and is well positioned

12:13.626 --> 12:15.536 align:middle
to handle tighter
monetary policy.

12:15.716 --> 12:17.876 align:middle
So, but I'll say, I do expect

12:17.876 --> 12:19.056 align:middle
that this will be
very challenging;

12:19.056 --> 12:20.046 align:middle
it's not going to be easy.

12:20.526 --> 12:22.166 align:middle
And it may well depend,
of course,

12:22.166 --> 12:24.446 align:middle
on events that are
not under our control.

12:25.086 --> 12:28.116 align:middle
But our job is to use our tools
to try to achieve that outcome,

12:28.116 --> 12:32.376 align:middle
and that's what we're
going to do.

12:32.666 --> 12:34.056 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

12:34.176 --> 12:35.256 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

12:35.256 --> 12:36.226 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

12:36.226 --> 12:38.236 align:middle
Thanks for taking my
question, Mr. Chairman.

12:38.236 --> 12:40.686 align:middle
You talked about using
50-basis-point rate hikes

12:40.686 --> 12:42.646 align:middle
or the possibility of
them in coming meetings.

12:43.116 --> 12:45.346 align:middle
Might there be something
larger than 50?

12:45.346 --> 12:47.936 align:middle
Is 75 or a percentage
point possible?

12:48.676 --> 12:51.126 align:middle
And perhaps you could walk
us through your calibration.

12:51.426 --> 12:54.796 align:middle
Why one month should-or one
meeting should we expect a 50?

12:54.796 --> 12:56.136 align:middle
Why something bigger?

12:56.226 --> 12:57.146 align:middle
Why something smaller?

12:57.536 --> 13:00.406 align:middle
What is the reasoning
for the level

13:00.446 --> 13:01.666 align:middle
of the amount of tightening?

13:01.706 --> 13:02.046 align:middle
Thank you.

13:02.946 --> 13:03.226 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:03.226 --> 13:08.346 align:middle
Sure. So a 75-basis-point
increase is not something the

13:08.346 --> 13:09.846 align:middle
Committee is actively
considering.

13:10.916 --> 13:13.536 align:middle
What we are doing is, we raised
[the funds rate by] 50 basis

13:13.536 --> 13:14.166 align:middle
points today.

13:14.576 --> 13:17.296 align:middle
And we've said that, again,
assuming that economic

13:17.296 --> 13:20.576 align:middle
and financial conditions evolve
in ways that are consistent

13:20.576 --> 13:23.596 align:middle
with our expectations, there's
a broad sense on the Committee

13:23.596 --> 13:26.556 align:middle
that additional 50-basis-point
increases should be on the table

13:26.626 --> 13:28.576 align:middle
for the next couple of meetings.

13:28.576 --> 13:31.656 align:middle
So we're going to make those
decisions at the meetings,

13:31.656 --> 13:33.936 align:middle
of course, and we'll be
paying close attention

13:33.936 --> 13:36.786 align:middle
to the incoming data and
the evolving outlook as well

13:36.786 --> 13:37.816 align:middle
as to financial conditions.

13:37.816 --> 13:40.476 align:middle
And, finally, of course, we will
be communicating to the public

13:40.776 --> 13:43.156 align:middle
about what our expectations
will be as they evolve.

13:43.596 --> 13:47.266 align:middle
So the test is really just
as I laid it out-economic

13:47.266 --> 13:49.246 align:middle
and financial conditions
evolving broadly

13:49.246 --> 13:50.516 align:middle
in line with expectations.

13:50.516 --> 13:52.096 align:middle
And, you know, I
think expectations are

13:52.636 --> 13:55.886 align:middle
that we'll start to see
inflation, you know,

13:55.886 --> 14:00.806 align:middle
flattening out-and not
necessarily declining yet,

14:00.806 --> 14:01.956 align:middle
but we'll see more evidence.

14:01.956 --> 14:03.316 align:middle
We've seen some evidence

14:03.316 --> 14:07.896 align:middle
that core PCE inflation is
perhaps either reaching a peak

14:07.896 --> 14:08.636 align:middle
or flattening out.

14:08.636 --> 14:10.866 align:middle
We'll want to know more
than just some evidence.

14:10.866 --> 14:11.736 align:middle
We'll want to really feel

14:11.736 --> 14:13.546 align:middle
like we're making
some progress there.

14:14.126 --> 14:17.016 align:middle
And we're going to
make these decisions,

14:17.016 --> 14:18.506 align:middle
and there'll be a
lot more information.

14:18.506 --> 14:22.016 align:middle
I just think we want to see that
information as we get there.

14:22.016 --> 14:24.426 align:middle
It's a very difficult
environment to try

14:24.426 --> 14:27.356 align:middle
to give forward guidance
60, 90 days in advance.

14:27.356 --> 14:28.956 align:middle
There are just so many
things that can happen

14:29.096 --> 14:30.536 align:middle
in the economy and
around the world.

14:31.146 --> 14:33.806 align:middle
So, you know, we're leaving
ourselves room to look

14:33.806 --> 14:36.066 align:middle
at the data and make a
decision as we get there.

14:36.066 --> 14:36.796 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

14:38.116 --> 14:41.616 align:middle
I'm sorry, but if
inflation is lower one month

14:41.616 --> 14:43.886 align:middle
and the unemployment rate
higher, would that be something

14:43.886 --> 14:47.906 align:middle
that we would calibrate
toward a lower increase

14:47.986 --> 14:49.116 align:middle
in the funds rate?

14:49.116 --> 14:49.746 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:49.746 --> 14:51.726 align:middle
I don't think the
one month is-no.

14:51.876 --> 14:54.906 align:middle
No. One month's reading
doesn't tell us much.

14:55.406 --> 14:57.166 align:middle
You know, we'd want
to see evidence

14:57.716 --> 15:00.376 align:middle
that inflation is
moving in a direction

15:00.376 --> 15:01.476 align:middle
that gives us more comfort.

15:02.076 --> 15:03.836 align:middle
As I said, we've
got two months now

15:03.836 --> 15:06.676 align:middle
where core inflation is a little
lower, but we're not looking

15:06.676 --> 15:09.286 align:middle
at that as a reason
to take some comfort.

15:09.286 --> 15:11.096 align:middle
You know, I think we
need to really see

15:11.696 --> 15:14.836 align:middle
that our expectation is being
fulfilled-that inflation,

15:14.836 --> 15:18.246 align:middle
in fact, is under control
and starting to come down.

15:18.816 --> 15:22.666 align:middle
But, again, it's not
like we would stop.

15:22.666 --> 15:25.106 align:middle
We would just go back to
25-basis-point increases.

15:25.156 --> 15:28.686 align:middle
It'll be a judgment call
when these meetings arrive.

15:28.686 --> 15:32.526 align:middle
But, again, our expectation is,
if we see what we expect to see,

15:32.526 --> 15:34.576 align:middle
then we would have
50-basis-point increases

15:34.576 --> 15:35.876 align:middle
on the table at the
next two meetings.

15:35.876 --> 15:36.486 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

15:37.486 --> 15:38.396 align:middle
Okay. Let's go to Colby.

15:40.486 --> 15:40.936 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

15:41.996 --> 15:42.576 align:middle
Thank you.

15:42.576 --> 15:44.166 align:middle
Colby Smith from
the Financial Times.

15:44.646 --> 15:47.516 align:middle
Given the expectation that
inflation will remain well

15:47.516 --> 15:49.546 align:middle
above the Fed's target
at year-end,

15:49.966 --> 15:53.166 align:middle
what constitutes a neutral
policy setting in terms

15:53.166 --> 15:54.196 align:middle
of the fed funds rate?

15:54.516 --> 15:57.116 align:middle
And to what extent is it
appropriate for policy

15:57.116 --> 15:59.886 align:middle
to move beyond that level
at some point this year?

16:00.346 --> 16:03.316 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:03.976 --> 16:05.246 align:middle
So-neutral.

16:06.126 --> 16:09.356 align:middle
When we talk about the neutral
rate, we're really talking

16:09.356 --> 16:13.686 align:middle
about the rate that neither
pushes economic activity higher

16:13.686 --> 16:14.606 align:middle
nor slows it down.

16:14.606 --> 16:15.816 align:middle
So it's a concept, really.

16:16.176 --> 16:18.566 align:middle
It's not something we can
identify with any precision.

16:18.876 --> 16:22.586 align:middle
So we estimate it within
broad bands of uncertainty.

16:22.816 --> 16:24.026 align:middle
And the current estimates

16:24.026 --> 16:26.346 align:middle
on the Committee are
sort of 2 to 3 percent.

16:26.386 --> 16:28.436 align:middle
And also, that's a
longer-run estimate.

16:28.436 --> 16:30.636 align:middle
That's an estimate
for an economy that's

16:30.636 --> 16:32.526 align:middle
at full employment and
2 percent inflation.

16:33.016 --> 16:36.166 align:middle
So, really, what we're doing is,

16:36.166 --> 16:40.046 align:middle
we're raising rates
expeditiously to what we see

16:40.046 --> 16:45.156 align:middle
as the broad range of
plausible levels of neutral.

16:45.666 --> 16:48.486 align:middle
But we know that there's not a
bright line drawn on the road

16:48.486 --> 16:49.596 align:middle
that tells us when we get there.

16:49.596 --> 16:52.016 align:middle
So we're going to be looking
at financial conditions, right?

16:52.016 --> 16:54.286 align:middle
Our policy affects
financial conditions,

16:54.286 --> 16:56.356 align:middle
and financial conditions
affect the economy.

16:56.756 --> 16:59.416 align:middle
So we're going to be looking at
the effect of our policy moves

16:59.416 --> 17:01.106 align:middle
on financial conditions.

17:01.536 --> 17:02.816 align:middle
Are they tightening
appropriately?

17:02.816 --> 17:03.786 align:middle
And then we're going
to be looking

17:03.786 --> 17:05.066 align:middle
at the effects on the economy.

17:05.066 --> 17:06.616 align:middle
And we're going to
be making a judgment

17:06.616 --> 17:09.756 align:middle
about whether we've done
enough to get us on a path

17:09.756 --> 17:10.946 align:middle
to restore price stability.

17:11.406 --> 17:11.886 align:middle
It's that.

17:11.886 --> 17:16.146 align:middle
So if that path happens to
involve levels that are higher

17:16.146 --> 17:18.426 align:middle
than estimates of neutral,
then we will not hesitate

17:18.626 --> 17:20.486 align:middle
to go to those levels.

17:20.606 --> 17:24.836 align:middle
We won't. But, again, there's
a sort of false precision

17:24.836 --> 17:27.946 align:middle
in the discussion that we as
policymakers don't really feel.

17:28.376 --> 17:30.776 align:middle
You know, you're going to
raise rates, and you're going

17:30.776 --> 17:34.576 align:middle
to be kind of inquiring how
that is affecting the economy

17:34.576 --> 17:35.826 align:middle
through financial conditions.

17:36.546 --> 17:40.116 align:middle
And, of course, if
higher rates are required,

17:40.116 --> 17:41.786 align:middle
then we won't hesitate
to deliver them.

17:42.246 --> 17:43.066 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:44.676 --> 17:45.626 align:middle
Neil. NEIL IRWIN.

17:46.586 --> 17:49.456 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

17:49.456 --> 17:50.436 align:middle
Neil Irwin with Axios.

17:50.776 --> 17:52.026 align:middle
Do you see evidence

17:52.076 --> 17:55.606 align:middle
that inflationary psychology
is changing in areas

17:55.606 --> 17:57.366 align:middle
like workers' wage demands,

17:57.506 --> 17:59.516 align:middle
businesses' willingness
to raise prices?

17:59.776 --> 18:02.496 align:middle
Do you see evidence that there
is a psychological shift going

18:02.496 --> 18:03.226 align:middle
on on inflation?

18:03.636 --> 18:05.866 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

18:05.916 --> 18:08.636 align:middle
We don't really see strong
evidence of that yet,

18:08.696 --> 18:10.836 align:middle
but that does not in any
way make us comfortable.

18:10.936 --> 18:12.396 align:middle
I think if you look

18:12.396 --> 18:14.236 align:middle
at short-term inflation
expectations,

18:14.716 --> 18:16.056 align:middle
they're quite elevated.

18:16.376 --> 18:17.556 align:middle
And you can look at
that and say, "Well,

18:17.556 --> 18:19.726 align:middle
that's because people expect
inflation to come down."

18:19.726 --> 18:21.846 align:middle
And, in fact, inflation
expectations [at longer

18:21.846 --> 18:23.096 align:middle
horizons] come down
fairly sharply.

18:23.956 --> 18:27.246 align:middle
Longer-term inflation
expectations have been

18:27.246 --> 18:30.336 align:middle
reasonably stable but have
moved up to-but only to levels

18:30.336 --> 18:33.076 align:middle
where they were in
2014, by some measures.

18:33.636 --> 18:35.716 align:middle
So you can look at that.

18:35.716 --> 18:38.356 align:middle
And I think that's a fair
description of the picture.

18:38.356 --> 18:40.916 align:middle
But it's really about the risks.

18:40.916 --> 18:42.726 align:middle
We don't see a wage-price
spiral.

18:42.976 --> 18:46.356 align:middle
We see that companies have
the ability to raise prices,

18:46.356 --> 18:48.726 align:middle
and they're doing that, but
there have been price shocks.

18:49.196 --> 18:54.346 align:middle
So I just think it takes you
back to the basic point-was

18:54.346 --> 18:55.256 align:middle
that we know we need

18:55.256 --> 19:00.036 align:middle
to expeditiously move our
policy rate up to ranges

19:00.146 --> 19:02.426 align:middle
of more normal, neutral levels.

19:02.426 --> 19:07.076 align:middle
And we need to look around
and keep going if we don't see

19:07.076 --> 19:09.086 align:middle
that financial conditions
have tightened adequately

19:09.086 --> 19:11.716 align:middle
or that the economy is
behaving in ways that suggest

19:11.756 --> 19:13.726 align:middle
that we're not where
we need to be.

19:14.256 --> 19:16.836 align:middle
So, again, you don't
see those things yet,

19:16.836 --> 19:18.226 align:middle
but I would say there's no basis

19:18.226 --> 19:19.976 align:middle
for feeling comfortable
about that.

19:20.556 --> 19:23.456 align:middle
It's a risk that we
simply can't run.

19:23.726 --> 19:26.976 align:middle
We can't allow a
wage-price spiral to happen.

19:27.136 --> 19:29.126 align:middle
And we can't allow
inflation expectations

19:29.126 --> 19:29.916 align:middle
to become unanchored.

19:30.446 --> 19:32.516 align:middle
It's just something that
we can't allow to happen,

19:32.516 --> 19:34.316 align:middle
and so we'll look
at it that way.

19:35.026 --> 19:35.586 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:35.826 --> 19:40.366 align:middle
Jeanna. JEANNA SMIALEK.

19:41.206 --> 19:42.496 align:middle
Great. Thanks, Chair Powell.

19:42.496 --> 19:43.976 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek with
the New York Times.

19:44.486 --> 19:47.096 align:middle
You mentioned in the
statement both the upside risks

19:47.156 --> 19:48.976 align:middle
to inflation from
Russia and China.

19:49.416 --> 19:51.756 align:middle
Obviously, those are very
much supply shocks rather

19:51.756 --> 19:52.726 align:middle
than demand side.

19:52.726 --> 19:54.836 align:middle
And I wonder what
you meant to convey

19:54.836 --> 19:59.056 align:middle
by adding them-I wonder what you
meant to convey by adding those.

19:59.056 --> 19:59.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:59.436 --> 20:04.486 align:middle
Well. So our tools don't
really work on supply shocks.

20:04.486 --> 20:05.956 align:middle
Our tools work on demand.

20:06.616 --> 20:10.366 align:middle
And to the extent we can't
affect, really, oil prices

20:10.366 --> 20:12.776 align:middle
or other commodity prices
or food prices and things

20:12.776 --> 20:16.256 align:middle
like that, so we
can't affect those.

20:16.256 --> 20:18.246 align:middle
But there's a job
to do on demand.

20:18.396 --> 20:20.396 align:middle
And that-you can see
that in the labor market,

20:20.396 --> 20:23.686 align:middle
where demand is substantially
in excess of supply of workers.

20:24.176 --> 20:25.976 align:middle
And you can see it in the
product markets as well.

20:26.736 --> 20:29.546 align:middle
But I guess I'm just pointing
out that-a couple of things.

20:29.626 --> 20:33.336 align:middle
For both the situation in
Ukraine and the situation

20:33.936 --> 20:38.136 align:middle
in China, they're likely to,
both, add to headline inflation.

20:38.526 --> 20:41.316 align:middle
And people are going to be
suffering from that, you know.

20:41.316 --> 20:46.706 align:middle
People almost suffer more from
food and energy shocks then,

20:46.796 --> 20:48.656 align:middle
but-even though they don't
actually tell us much

20:48.656 --> 20:49.786 align:middle
about the future path.

20:49.786 --> 20:52.316 align:middle
So the second thing is
that they're both capable

20:52.316 --> 20:58.486 align:middle
of preventing further progress
in supply chains healing

20:59.096 --> 21:01.646 align:middle
or even making supply
chains temporarily worse.

21:01.646 --> 21:06.246 align:middle
So they're going to
weigh on the process

21:06.296 --> 21:10.626 align:middle
of global supply chain
healing, which is going

21:10.626 --> 21:12.306 align:middle
to affect broader
inflation, too.

21:12.946 --> 21:16.176 align:middle
So, in a way, they're two
further negative shocks

21:16.176 --> 21:17.866 align:middle
that have hit really
in the last,

21:18.496 --> 21:20.126 align:middle
you know, 60 days, 90 days.

21:21.206 --> 21:21.606 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:22.226 --> 21:27.866 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

21:27.866 --> 21:29.536 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
from Politico.

21:29.536 --> 21:31.726 align:middle
I want to follow up on that,

21:31.796 --> 21:33.986 align:middle
because you all have
obviously highlighted

21:33.986 --> 21:35.066 align:middle
that there are both supply

21:35.066 --> 21:37.386 align:middle
and demand issues at
play in inflation.

21:37.766 --> 21:39.316 align:middle
And I'm just wondering,
you know,

21:39.316 --> 21:43.146 align:middle
if these supply chain issues
continue because of Russia,

21:43.146 --> 21:44.896 align:middle
because of China, or just

21:44.896 --> 21:47.276 align:middle
because these things
take a while to work out,

21:47.866 --> 21:52.076 align:middle
does getting back down to
your 2 percent mandate require

21:52.076 --> 21:54.166 align:middle
that the supply chain
issues get resolved,

21:54.166 --> 21:57.066 align:middle
since you can only handle
the demand side, as you said?

21:57.066 --> 22:01.696 align:middle
Or will you have to crimp
demand maybe even further

22:01.696 --> 22:03.806 align:middle
if the supply chain issues
don't resolve themselves,

22:03.806 --> 22:05.526 align:middle
in order to try and get
inflation back down to

22:05.526 --> 22:06.266 align:middle
where you want it to be?

22:06.526 --> 22:06.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:07.026 --> 22:09.286 align:middle
So, you know, I'll just
say: For now, we're focused

22:09.286 --> 22:11.046 align:middle
on doing the job we
need to do on demand,

22:11.046 --> 22:12.466 align:middle
and there's plenty
to be done there.

22:12.966 --> 22:15.916 align:middle
Again, if you look at it, it's
essentially almost two to one,

22:15.916 --> 22:18.106 align:middle
job vacancies to
unemployed people.

22:18.106 --> 22:19.326 align:middle
There's a lot of excess demand.

22:19.326 --> 22:24.426 align:middle
There're more than 5 million
more employed plus job openings

22:24.426 --> 22:25.956 align:middle
than there are the size
of the labor force.

22:25.956 --> 22:30.906 align:middle
So there's an imbalance there
that we have to do our work on.

22:31.336 --> 22:35.216 align:middle
A very difficult situation-you
know, you would look

22:35.216 --> 22:36.396 align:middle
at core inflation,

22:36.396 --> 22:39.846 align:middle
which wouldn't include the
commodity price shocks.

22:40.066 --> 22:42.836 align:middle
And, you know, that's one of the
reasons we would tend to focus

22:42.836 --> 22:45.696 align:middle
on that, because we can have
more of an effect on that,

22:45.736 --> 22:47.456 align:middle
but it would be a very
difficult situation.

22:48.026 --> 22:49.596 align:middle
I mean, we have to be sure

22:49.596 --> 22:52.366 align:middle
that inflation expectations
remain anchored.

22:53.106 --> 22:55.376 align:middle
And I mean, that's
part of our job, too,

22:55.376 --> 22:57.086 align:middle
so we'd be watching
that carefully.

22:57.086 --> 23:00.866 align:middle
And it puts any central bank
in a very difficult situation.

23:01.326 --> 23:02.566 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:02.746 --> 23:06.356 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

23:06.726 --> 23:08.076 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

23:08.076 --> 23:09.946 align:middle
And thanks, and nice to be back.

23:10.006 --> 23:11.856 align:middle
So two questions.

23:11.976 --> 23:12.736 align:middle
One quick one.

23:13.266 --> 23:15.916 align:middle
You've cited the 1.9 to
1 figure so often now,

23:15.916 --> 23:18.026 align:middle
I've got to ask you, what
would be a good figure there?

23:18.026 --> 23:19.946 align:middle
What would you like to see
that come down to to think

23:19.946 --> 23:20.576 align:middle
that you're in sort

23:20.576 --> 23:23.336 align:middle
of a noninflationary
vacancy-to-unemployed rate?

23:23.506 --> 23:25.666 align:middle
And, secondly, on
help from inflation,

23:25.666 --> 23:28.076 align:middle
how much are you
counting on wealth effects

23:28.146 --> 23:29.796 align:middle
through stock market channels?

23:29.796 --> 23:32.726 align:middle
Equity markets broadly down
quite a bit since late fall,

23:32.726 --> 23:34.766 align:middle
first of the year-how
are you mapping

23:34.766 --> 23:36.366 align:middle
that into household consumption?

23:36.556 --> 23:37.596 align:middle
Have they come down enough?

23:37.686 --> 23:41.216 align:middle
Do you need another leg down
in equity values to think

23:41.266 --> 23:44.146 align:middle
that households are
going to stop spending

23:44.146 --> 23:45.746 align:middle
at the rate we need
them to stop spending?

23:46.636 --> 23:47.406 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:47.406 --> 23:51.046 align:middle
Okay, so in terms of the
vacancies-to-unemployment ratio,

23:51.276 --> 23:52.876 align:middle
we don't have a goal in mind.

23:53.486 --> 23:55.166 align:middle
There's no specific
number that we're saying,

23:55.166 --> 23:56.016 align:middle
"We've got to get to that."

23:56.306 --> 23:58.016 align:middle
It's really, you've
got to get to a place

23:58.016 --> 24:00.976 align:middle
where the labor market
appears to be more in balance.

24:01.416 --> 24:05.016 align:middle
And that depends not only on the
level of those things-it also,

24:05.106 --> 24:08.266 align:middle
it depends on how well
the matching functions

24:08.266 --> 24:09.526 align:middle
in the labor markets
are working.

24:09.696 --> 24:13.206 align:middle
Because, you know, the longer
these expansions go on,

24:13.206 --> 24:14.786 align:middle
you can get very
efficient with all of that,

24:14.786 --> 24:15.966 align:middle
and the Beveridge
curve shifts out.

24:15.966 --> 24:18.626 align:middle
And that also tends
to, you know, to help.

24:19.136 --> 24:20.376 align:middle
So there isn't a
specific number.

24:20.376 --> 24:23.026 align:middle
I will say we were-you know,
I think, I think when we got

24:23.026 --> 24:26.176 align:middle
to one-to-one in the, you
know, in the late teens,

24:26.176 --> 24:27.276 align:middle
we thought that was
a pretty good number.

24:27.276 --> 24:29.466 align:middle
But, again, we're not shooting
for any particular number.

24:29.466 --> 24:30.876 align:middle
What we'd like to
see is progress,

24:31.156 --> 24:32.916 align:middle
but we're not really
looking at that.

24:33.156 --> 24:34.956 align:middle
That's an intermediate variable.

24:34.956 --> 24:36.686 align:middle
We're looking at wages,
and we're looking at,

24:36.686 --> 24:37.676 align:middle
ultimately, inflation.

24:38.476 --> 24:41.206 align:middle
So, you know, there
are a bunch of channels

24:41.206 --> 24:42.596 align:middle
through which policy works.

24:42.896 --> 24:44.436 align:middle
You can think of
it as, you know,

24:44.436 --> 24:47.256 align:middle
interest-sensitive spending,
and then you can think

24:47.256 --> 24:50.746 align:middle
of another big one as
asset values broadly.

24:51.266 --> 24:54.126 align:middle
And, you know, there're
big models with, you know,

24:54.126 --> 24:56.616 align:middle
a lot of different channels
that are related to that.

24:56.746 --> 25:00.006 align:middle
You know, we don't focus on any
one market-the equity market

25:00.006 --> 25:03.896 align:middle
or the housing market-we focus
on financial conditions broadly.

25:04.276 --> 25:07.476 align:middle
So we wouldn't be targeting
any one market, as you suggest,

25:07.476 --> 25:11.036 align:middle
for going up or down or
taking a view on whether it's

25:11.036 --> 25:12.316 align:middle
at a good level or a bad level.

25:12.316 --> 25:14.426 align:middle
We just would be looking
at very broad measures

25:14.886 --> 25:17.496 align:middle
of financial conditions-all the
different financial conditions

25:17.496 --> 25:20.236 align:middle
indexes, for example,
which include equity.

25:20.236 --> 25:22.216 align:middle
But they also include
debt and other,

25:22.216 --> 25:25.156 align:middle
many other things-credit
spreads, things like that-too.

25:25.156 --> 25:27.246 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

25:27.896 --> 25:31.026 align:middle
Rachel. RACHEL SIEGEL.

25:32.246 --> 25:36.046 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

25:36.046 --> 25:37.856 align:middle
Rachel Siegel here from
the Washington Post.

25:38.356 --> 25:40.746 align:middle
To follow up from your message
from the very beginning,

25:41.076 --> 25:42.656 align:middle
what is your message
to the American people

25:42.656 --> 25:45.486 align:middle
about when they will start
to feel the effects of, say,

25:45.536 --> 25:47.756 align:middle
a 50-basis-point rate
hike or multiple hikes?

25:48.166 --> 25:51.146 align:middle
How do you explain to them what
that does to their grocery bill

25:51.146 --> 25:52.816 align:middle
or their rent or their gas bill?

25:53.096 --> 25:53.806 align:middle
Thank you.

25:54.906 --> 25:55.726 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:55.976 --> 25:59.376 align:middle
So, first, the first thing
to say is that we understand.

25:59.876 --> 26:01.606 align:middle
And some of us are old
enough to have lived

26:01.606 --> 26:03.716 align:middle
through high inflation,
and many aren't.

26:04.156 --> 26:05.946 align:middle
But it's very unpleasant.

26:06.126 --> 26:11.126 align:middle
It's just something people
don't-when they experience it

26:11.126 --> 26:13.586 align:middle
for the first time, you're
paying more for the same thing.

26:14.036 --> 26:17.276 align:middle
If you're a normal
economic person,

26:17.276 --> 26:21.706 align:middle
then you probably don't have
that much extra to spend.

26:21.786 --> 26:25.096 align:middle
And it's immediately hitting
your spending on groceries, on,

26:25.826 --> 26:28.366 align:middle
you know, on gasoline, on
energy, and things like that.

26:28.366 --> 26:29.876 align:middle
So we understand
the pain involved.

26:30.456 --> 26:33.116 align:middle
So how do you get out of that?

26:33.316 --> 26:38.546 align:middle
And it's our job to
make sure that inflation

26:38.546 --> 26:41.856 align:middle
of that unpleasant, high
nature doesn't get entrenched

26:41.856 --> 26:42.366 align:middle
in the economy.

26:42.366 --> 26:43.396 align:middle
That's what we're here for,

26:43.396 --> 26:45.476 align:middle
one of the main things we're
here for-perhaps the most

26:45.476 --> 26:46.686 align:middle
fundamental thing
we're here for.

26:46.946 --> 26:49.486 align:middle
And the way we do that
is, we try to get supply

26:49.486 --> 26:54.866 align:middle
and demand back in sync with
each other, back in balance,

26:55.566 --> 26:57.896 align:middle
so that the economy
is under less stress

26:57.976 --> 26:59.656 align:middle
and inflation will go down.

26:59.756 --> 27:02.986 align:middle
Now, the process of getting
there involves higher rates-so

27:02.986 --> 27:03.866 align:middle
higher mortgage rates,

27:03.866 --> 27:05.476 align:middle
higher borrowing rates,
and things like that.

27:05.516 --> 27:09.506 align:middle
So it's not going to
be pleasant either.

27:09.506 --> 27:11.696 align:middle
But in the end, everyone
is better off.

27:11.806 --> 27:15.266 align:middle
Everyone- particularly
people on fixed incomes

27:15.906 --> 27:16.776 align:middle
and at the lower part

27:16.776 --> 27:20.336 align:middle
of the income distribution are
better off with stable prices.

27:20.476 --> 27:24.026 align:middle
And so we need to do everything
we can to restore stable prices.

27:24.666 --> 27:28.046 align:middle
We'll do it as quickly
and effectively as we can.

27:28.046 --> 27:31.386 align:middle
We think we have a
good chance to do it

27:32.116 --> 27:35.316 align:middle
without a significant increase
in unemployment or, you know,

27:35.316 --> 27:36.406 align:middle
a really sharp slowdown.

27:36.436 --> 27:39.656 align:middle
But, ultimately, we think about
the medium and longer term,

27:39.756 --> 27:41.166 align:middle
and everyone will be better off

27:41.286 --> 27:43.416 align:middle
if we can get this job
done-the sooner, the better.

27:43.416 --> 27:44.066 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:44.656 --> 27:45.286 align:middle
Thank you.

27:45.286 --> 27:47.436 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

27:48.036 --> 27:49.066 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

27:49.096 --> 27:50.996 align:middle
Edward Lawrence with the
Fox Business Network.

27:50.996 --> 27:54.636 align:middle
So you've talked in the past
about consumer spending and how

27:54.636 --> 27:56.336 align:middle
that drives the economy.

27:56.776 --> 27:58.966 align:middle
Are you concerned, with this
high level of inflation,

27:59.306 --> 28:02.746 align:middle
that the consumer will stop
spending-and what's the level

28:02.746 --> 28:04.756 align:middle
of your concern-pushing
us into a recession?

28:04.876 --> 28:06.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:07.226 --> 28:13.066 align:middle
So the economy is
doing fairly well.

28:15.096 --> 28:18.096 align:middle
We expect growth to
be solid this year.

28:18.876 --> 28:22.956 align:middle
And we see, you know, household
spending and business investment

28:23.806 --> 28:25.776 align:middle
as fairly strong-and even
in the first quarter,

28:25.776 --> 28:28.506 align:middle
which was relatively slow
on some other fronts.

28:29.056 --> 28:31.486 align:middle
So, and the labor market-if
you look at the labor market,

28:32.236 --> 28:34.356 align:middle
for people who are out of work
and looking, there are lots

28:34.356 --> 28:39.056 align:middle
of job opportunities for-wages
are moving up and at rates

28:39.056 --> 28:40.656 align:middle
that haven't been seen
in quite a long time.

28:40.656 --> 28:46.506 align:middle
So it's a good time to be a
worker looking to, you know,

28:46.506 --> 28:50.086 align:middle
either change jobs or get a wage
increase in your current job.

28:50.086 --> 28:53.726 align:middle
So it's a strong economy,
and nothing about it suggests

28:54.166 --> 28:57.366 align:middle
that it's close to, or
vulnerable to, a recession.

28:57.756 --> 29:00.326 align:middle
Now, of course, given
events around the world

29:00.556 --> 29:04.356 align:middle
and fading fiscal policy
effects and higher rates,

29:05.066 --> 29:08.966 align:middle
you could see some
slower economic activity.

29:08.966 --> 29:11.256 align:middle
Certainly, it will not be-last
year was an extraordinarily

29:11.806 --> 29:14.516 align:middle
strong growth year as we
recovered from the pandemic.

29:14.516 --> 29:16.326 align:middle
As I mentioned, growth
[was] over 5 percent.

29:16.376 --> 29:20.166 align:middle
But most forecasters have
growth this year at, you know,

29:20.166 --> 29:22.316 align:middle
at a solid pace above 2 percent.

29:22.316 --> 29:22.906 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

29:22.906 --> 29:26.336 align:middle
But we've talked with economists
who have advised Democrats

29:26.336 --> 29:29.716 align:middle
and Republican Presidents
who both said that the Fed is

29:29.716 --> 29:31.376 align:middle
so far behind the
curve on inflation

29:31.376 --> 29:33.916 align:middle
that a recession is inevitable.

29:34.686 --> 29:35.476 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:35.556 --> 29:39.386 align:middle
So, and as I said, I think
we have a good chance

29:39.806 --> 29:44.296 align:middle
to restore price stability
without a recession-without,

29:44.516 --> 29:45.716 align:middle
you know, a severe downturn;

29:45.716 --> 29:47.816 align:middle
without materially high,
higher unemployment.

29:48.146 --> 29:49.426 align:middle
And I mentioned the
reasons for that.

29:49.686 --> 29:52.306 align:middle
So I see a strong economy now.

29:52.306 --> 29:54.426 align:middle
I see a very strong labor
market, for example.

29:55.516 --> 29:58.366 align:middle
Businesses can't find the people
to hire-they can't find them.

29:58.486 --> 30:01.206 align:middle
So, typically, in a recession,
you would have unemployment.

30:01.476 --> 30:02.986 align:middle
Now you have surplus demand.

30:03.286 --> 30:05.096 align:middle
So there should be
room, in principle,

30:05.626 --> 30:07.256 align:middle
to reduce that surplus demand

30:07.256 --> 30:08.916 align:middle
without putting people
out of work.

30:09.236 --> 30:11.806 align:middle
The issue will come that
we don't have precision

30:11.806 --> 30:12.806 align:middle
surgical tools.

30:13.316 --> 30:16.366 align:middle
We have, essentially, interest
rates, the balance sheet,

30:16.366 --> 30:19.616 align:middle
and forward guidance, and
they're famously blunt tools.

30:19.676 --> 30:22.006 align:middle
They're not capable
of surgical precision.

30:22.006 --> 30:23.996 align:middle
So I would agree.

30:24.376 --> 30:25.816 align:middle
No one thinks this will be easy,

30:25.936 --> 30:27.416 align:middle
no one thinks it's
straightforward,

30:27.946 --> 30:30.156 align:middle
but there is certainly a
plausible path to this,

30:30.246 --> 30:33.416 align:middle
and I do think we've got
a good chance to do that.

30:33.416 --> 30:35.406 align:middle
And, you know, our job is not

30:35.406 --> 30:37.776 align:middle
to rate the chances-it
is to try to achieve it.

30:37.776 --> 30:38.876 align:middle
So that's what we're doing.

30:39.636 --> 30:40.816 align:middle
There are a range
of opinions, though,

30:40.816 --> 30:42.686 align:middle
and that's only appropriate.

30:43.386 --> 30:43.906 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

30:43.906 --> 30:44.566 align:middle
Steve Dorsey.

30:45.076 --> 30:46.236 align:middle
STEVE DORSEY.

30:47.296 --> 30:48.186 align:middle
Thanks. Steve Dorsey, CBS.

30:48.186 --> 30:52.276 align:middle
You mentioned earlier, just
now, fading fiscal policy.

30:52.966 --> 30:57.016 align:middle
Do you feel that the Fed
has been supported enough

30:57.176 --> 31:00.236 align:middle
from policies at the White
House and in Congress

31:00.336 --> 31:01.516 align:middle
in combating inflation?

31:02.066 --> 31:02.906 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:03.106 --> 31:05.296 align:middle
You know, it's really the Fed

31:05.296 --> 31:07.426 align:middle
that has responsibility
for price stability.

31:07.616 --> 31:12.546 align:middle
And we take whatever
arrives at the Fed in terms

31:12.546 --> 31:15.416 align:middle
of fiscal activity,
we take it as a given,

31:15.416 --> 31:18.986 align:middle
and we don't evaluate
it-it's not our job, really.

31:18.986 --> 31:20.556 align:middle
We don't have an
oversight function there.

31:21.176 --> 31:24.736 align:middle
And we look at it as our
job, given all the factors

31:24.736 --> 31:25.956 align:middle
that are happening, to try

31:25.956 --> 31:28.366 align:middle
to sustain maximum
employment and price stability.

31:28.366 --> 31:33.596 align:middle
So if Congress or the
Administration has ways to help

31:33.596 --> 31:35.606 align:middle
with inflation, I
would encourage that,

31:35.606 --> 31:38.056 align:middle
but I'm not going to get
into making recommendations

31:38.056 --> 31:39.106 align:middle
or anything like that.

31:39.106 --> 31:41.146 align:middle
It's not, it's really not
our role-we need to stay

31:41.146 --> 31:43.466 align:middle
in our lane and do our job.

31:43.466 --> 31:48.026 align:middle
When we get inflation back
under control, then maybe I can,

31:48.516 --> 31:50.336 align:middle
you know, give other
people advice [laughter].

31:50.336 --> 31:53.956 align:middle
Right now, we need to focus on,
just focus on doing our job.

31:54.946 --> 31:55.766 align:middle
And I'll stick to that.

31:55.766 --> 31:56.456 align:middle
Stick in our lane.

31:57.046 --> 31:57.976 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:58.086 --> 32:01.276 align:middle
Steve Matthews.

32:01.976 --> 32:03.376 align:middle
STEVE MATTHEWS.

32:03.376 --> 32:04.936 align:middle
Steve Matthews with
Bloomberg News.

32:05.596 --> 32:09.696 align:middle
A number of your colleagues have
said that the rates will need

32:09.696 --> 32:13.406 align:middle
to go above neutral into
a restrictive territory

32:13.406 --> 32:14.706 align:middle
to bring down inflation.

32:15.406 --> 32:16.796 align:middle
One, do you agree with that?

32:17.426 --> 32:22.636 align:middle
And, two, you've recently spoken
in great praise of Paul Volcker,

32:23.376 --> 32:25.266 align:middle
who had the courage
to bring inflation

32:25.266 --> 32:28.376 align:middle
down with recessions
in the 1980s.

32:28.696 --> 32:32.486 align:middle
And while it's certainly not
your desire-the soft landing is

32:32.816 --> 32:37.656 align:middle
the big hope of everyone-would
this FOMC have the courage

32:38.106 --> 32:40.666 align:middle
to endure recessions to
bring inflation down if

32:40.666 --> 32:42.746 align:middle
that were the only
way necessary?

32:43.216 --> 32:43.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:44.306 --> 32:47.546 align:middle
So I think it's certainly
possible that we'll need

32:47.546 --> 32:50.746 align:middle
to move policy to levels
that we see as restrictive

32:50.746 --> 32:52.086 align:middle
as opposed to just neutral.

32:52.876 --> 32:54.836 align:middle
We can't know that today.

32:55.056 --> 32:56.736 align:middle
That decision is not
in front of us today.

32:57.186 --> 32:59.016 align:middle
If we do conclude that
we need to do that,

32:59.146 --> 33:00.656 align:middle
then we won't hesitate to do it.

33:00.986 --> 33:03.576 align:middle
I'll say again, there's
no bright line, you know,

33:03.576 --> 33:04.666 align:middle
that you're stepping over.

33:04.726 --> 33:08.696 align:middle
You're really looking at
what our policy stance is

33:08.956 --> 33:10.816 align:middle
and what the market
is forecasting for it.

33:10.816 --> 33:12.166 align:middle
You're looking at
financial conditions

33:12.446 --> 33:14.966 align:middle
and how that's affecting the
economy and making a judgment.

33:15.526 --> 33:17.536 align:middle
You know, we won't be
arguing about whose model

33:17.536 --> 33:20.046 align:middle
of the neutral rate is
better than the other one.

33:20.046 --> 33:24.466 align:middle
It's much more about a practical
application of our policy tools.

33:24.746 --> 33:26.476 align:middle
And we're absolutely
prepared to do that.

33:26.636 --> 33:28.416 align:middle
It-wouldn't hesitate if
that's what it takes.

33:29.146 --> 33:32.976 align:middle
So I am, of course-who isn't
an admirer of Paul Volcker?

33:32.976 --> 33:35.026 align:middle
I shouldn't be singled
out in this respect.

33:35.026 --> 33:37.686 align:middle
But I knew him just a little bit

33:37.686 --> 33:39.756 align:middle
and have tremendous
admiration for him.

33:39.966 --> 33:42.886 align:middle
And I-But I would phrase it
this way: He had the courage

33:42.886 --> 33:44.436 align:middle
to do what he thought
was the right thing.

33:45.276 --> 33:46.036 align:middle
That's what it was.

33:46.166 --> 33:47.596 align:middle
It wasn't a particular thing.

33:47.596 --> 33:50.966 align:middle
It was that he always did-he
always did-what he thought was

33:50.966 --> 33:51.296 align:middle
the right thing.

33:51.296 --> 33:53.266 align:middle
If you read his last
autobiography,

33:53.266 --> 33:54.146 align:middle
that really comes through.

33:54.636 --> 33:56.636 align:middle
So that's [what] the test is.

33:56.936 --> 33:58.996 align:middle
It isn't, will we do
one particular thing?

33:59.216 --> 34:00.776 align:middle
I would say, we do see,

34:00.776 --> 34:02.936 align:middle
though-we see restoring
price stability

34:02.936 --> 34:07.196 align:middle
as absolutely essential for
the country in coming years.

34:07.946 --> 34:09.256 align:middle
Without price stability,

34:09.806 --> 34:11.646 align:middle
the economy doesn't work
for anybody, really.

34:11.876 --> 34:14.626 align:middle
And so it's really essential,

34:14.626 --> 34:15.936 align:middle
particularly for
the labor market.

34:15.936 --> 34:18.076 align:middle
If you think about it, like
if you look at the last cycle,

34:18.686 --> 34:22.046 align:middle
we had a very, very-longest
expansion cycle

34:22.096 --> 34:24.186 align:middle
in our recorded history.

34:24.506 --> 34:27.586 align:middle
In the last two, three
years, you had the benefits

34:27.586 --> 34:30.576 align:middle
of this tight labor
market going to people

34:30.576 --> 34:31.806 align:middle
in the lower quartiles.

34:32.016 --> 34:34.916 align:middle
And it was, you know, racial,
wealth, and income-not wealth,

34:34.916 --> 34:38.116 align:middle
but income gaps were
coming down, wage gaps.

34:38.116 --> 34:40.036 align:middle
So it's a really great thing.

34:40.036 --> 34:41.656 align:middle
We'd all love to get
back to that place.

34:41.706 --> 34:43.536 align:middle
But to get back to
anything like that place,

34:43.536 --> 34:44.486 align:middle
you need price stability.

34:44.976 --> 34:46.786 align:middle
So, basically, we've been hit

34:46.786 --> 34:50.236 align:middle
by historically large
inflationary shocks

34:50.236 --> 34:51.176 align:middle
since the pandemic.

34:51.736 --> 34:54.896 align:middle
This isn't anything
like regular business.

34:54.896 --> 34:58.206 align:middle
This is, we have a pandemic, we
have the highest unemployment,

34:58.766 --> 35:01.316 align:middle
you know, in-since
the Depression.

35:01.906 --> 35:03.706 align:middle
Then we have this
outsized response

35:03.706 --> 35:05.736 align:middle
from fiscal policy
and monetary policy.

35:05.736 --> 35:06.666 align:middle
Then we have inflation.

35:06.966 --> 35:10.566 align:middle
Then we have a war in Ukraine,
which is cutting the commodity,

35:11.256 --> 35:12.576 align:middle
you know, patch in half.

35:12.896 --> 35:14.626 align:middle
And now we have these
shutdowns in China.

35:14.626 --> 35:16.826 align:middle
So it's been a series
of inflationary shocks

35:16.826 --> 35:18.106 align:middle
that are really different

35:18.106 --> 35:20.666 align:middle
from anything people
have seen in 40 years.

35:21.266 --> 35:23.766 align:middle
So we have to look
through that and look

35:23.766 --> 35:25.696 align:middle
at the economy that's
coming out the other side.

35:26.226 --> 35:30.416 align:middle
And we need to somehow find
price stability out of this.

35:31.076 --> 35:33.556 align:middle
And it's obviously going to
be very challenging, I think,

35:33.556 --> 35:36.606 align:middle
because you do have, you
know, numerous supply shocks,

35:36.606 --> 35:38.556 align:middle
which are famously
difficult to deal with.

35:39.016 --> 35:42.306 align:middle
So I guess that's
how I think about it.

35:42.306 --> 35:43.316 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

35:43.316 --> 35:44.136 align:middle
Chris Rugaber.

35:45.846 --> 35:47.376 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

35:47.376 --> 35:47.896 align:middle
Thank you.

35:47.896 --> 35:49.556 align:middle
Chris Rugaber, Associated Press.

35:49.886 --> 35:53.506 align:middle
Earlier, you just said that
if necessary-I think were the

35:53.506 --> 35:56.486 align:middle
words-that you would,
or if it, if it turned

35:56.486 --> 35:58.716 align:middle
out to be necessary, or
you said that it's possible

35:58.716 --> 36:01.466 align:middle
that we'll need to move
policy to restrictive levels.

36:01.506 --> 36:05.146 align:middle
Given where inflation is and the
hot economy is-or, certainly,

36:05.146 --> 36:05.976 align:middle
the hot labor market,

36:05.976 --> 36:08.016 align:middle
as you described it-why
still the hesitation?

36:08.016 --> 36:13.236 align:middle
I mean, what else do you need to
see in order to determine that?

36:13.946 --> 36:15.486 align:middle
Wouldn't the Fed
naturally be looking to go

36:15.486 --> 36:17.356 align:middle
to a restrictive
level at this point?

36:17.866 --> 36:18.176 align:middle
Thank you.

36:18.176 --> 36:19.226 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:19.226 --> 36:20.956 align:middle
So I didn't-if I
said "necessary,"

36:20.956 --> 36:22.016 align:middle
I meant to say "appropriate."

36:22.176 --> 36:24.666 align:middle
We're not going to be erecting
a high barrier for this.

36:24.666 --> 36:26.156 align:middle
It's more, if we think
it's appropriate.

36:26.546 --> 36:30.096 align:middle
You know, the point is, we're a
very long way from neutral now.

36:30.616 --> 36:32.176 align:middle
We're moving there
expeditiously,

36:32.406 --> 36:33.536 align:middle
and we'll continue to do so.

36:34.296 --> 36:38.046 align:middle
And we can't make that
decision, really, today.

36:38.046 --> 36:45.006 align:middle
The decision for about how
high to go will be on the table

36:45.216 --> 36:47.786 align:middle
to be made when we
reach neutral.

36:48.416 --> 36:52.056 align:middle
And, you know, I expect we'll
get there expeditiously,

36:52.056 --> 36:52.686 align:middle
as I've mentioned.

36:52.836 --> 36:56.726 align:middle
So it's not that we
don't want to-making

36:56.726 --> 36:58.456 align:middle
that decision today wouldn't
really mean anything.

36:59.006 --> 37:03.576 align:middle
But I'll say again, if we do
believe that it's appropriate

37:04.136 --> 37:05.766 align:middle
to go to those levels,
we won't hesitate.

37:06.176 --> 37:07.446 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

37:07.656 --> 37:09.256 align:middle
Michael McKee.

37:10.326 --> 37:10.886 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

37:12.056 --> 37:14.736 align:middle
Mike McKee from Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

37:15.096 --> 37:19.496 align:middle
The balance sheet-why did you
decide to wait until June 1st

37:19.496 --> 37:25.176 align:middle
to begin letting securities roll
off and not immediately start

37:25.296 --> 37:26.636 align:middle
in the middle of
this month, say?

37:27.156 --> 37:30.446 align:middle
And do you have another,
a newer or a better,

37:30.446 --> 37:33.356 align:middle
estimate for the
monetary policy impact

37:33.886 --> 37:35.786 align:middle
of letting the balance
sheet decline?

37:35.786 --> 37:38.846 align:middle
And then, finally, I'm just
curious why you felt the need

37:38.846 --> 37:42.406 align:middle
to address the American people
at the top of your remarks?

37:42.406 --> 37:44.816 align:middle
Are you concerned
about Fed credibility

37:44.816 --> 37:45.706 align:middle
with the American people?

37:45.916 --> 37:46.646 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:46.906 --> 37:47.836 align:middle
So, why June 1?

37:47.836 --> 37:51.056 align:middle
It was just, pick a date,
you know, and that happened

37:51.056 --> 37:52.946 align:middle
to be the date that we picked.

37:52.946 --> 37:54.976 align:middle
It was-nothing magic about it.

37:54.976 --> 37:58.746 align:middle
You know, it's not going to have
any macroeconomic significance

37:58.746 --> 37:59.246 align:middle
over time.

37:59.356 --> 38:00.006 align:middle
We just picked that.

38:00.006 --> 38:02.776 align:middle
Sometimes we publish these
calendars on the first day

38:02.776 --> 38:05.506 align:middle
of the month, and
that's what we're doing.

38:05.816 --> 38:07.046 align:middle
I wouldn't read anything
into it.

38:07.616 --> 38:08.836 align:middle
In terms of the effect, I mean,

38:08.926 --> 38:12.196 align:middle
I would just stress how
uncertain the effect is

38:12.286 --> 38:14.676 align:middle
of shrinking the balance sheet.

38:15.246 --> 38:19.606 align:middle
You know, we run these
models-and everyone does

38:19.686 --> 38:23.256 align:middle
in this field-and make
estimates of what will be,

38:23.256 --> 38:27.546 align:middle
how do you measure, you
know, a certain quantum

38:27.826 --> 38:31.866 align:middle
of balance sheet shrinkage
compared to quantitative easing?

38:32.096 --> 38:34.556 align:middle
And, you know, these
are very uncertain.

38:34.556 --> 38:36.056 align:middle
I really can't be any clearer.

38:36.056 --> 38:37.466 align:middle
There won't be any
clearer [estimates].

38:37.926 --> 38:41.206 align:middle
You know, people
estimate that, broadly,

38:41.206 --> 38:45.776 align:middle
on the path we're on-and
this will be taken, probably,

38:45.776 --> 38:50.326 align:middle
too seriously-but sort of ¼
percent, one rate increase

38:50.326 --> 38:51.996 align:middle
over the course of
a year at this pace.

38:52.516 --> 38:55.506 align:middle
But I would just say,
with very wide uncertainty

38:55.506 --> 38:56.516 align:middle
bands-very wide.

38:57.006 --> 38:57.816 align:middle
We don't really know.

38:57.816 --> 38:59.456 align:middle
There are other estimates
that are much smaller

38:59.456 --> 39:00.076 align:middle
than that, by the way.

39:00.076 --> 39:01.386 align:middle
And some of you may
read about that.

39:01.686 --> 39:03.926 align:middle
That's kind of a
mainstream estimate.

39:04.386 --> 39:09.846 align:middle
We know that it is part of
returning to more normal,

39:09.846 --> 39:11.826 align:middle
more neutral financial
conditions.

39:12.046 --> 39:15.466 align:middle
And, you know, our
strategy is to set up a plan

39:15.466 --> 39:20.636 align:middle
and have it operate and
really have, you know,

39:20.636 --> 39:24.396 align:middle
have the interest rate be the
active tool of monetary policy.

39:25.436 --> 39:27.176 align:middle
In terms of speaking
to the American people,

39:27.346 --> 39:32.306 align:middle
so I feel like sometimes I
just want to remind us, really,

39:32.846 --> 39:35.966 align:middle
that that's who we work
for, and that it's inflation

39:36.096 --> 39:38.176 align:middle
that people are feeling
all over the country.

39:38.176 --> 39:40.076 align:middle
And it's very important
that they know

39:40.526 --> 39:42.986 align:middle
that we know how painful it is

39:42.986 --> 39:45.366 align:middle
and that we are working
hard on fixing it.

39:45.366 --> 39:48.156 align:middle
I thought it was quite
important to do that.

39:48.156 --> 39:51.956 align:middle
And so that was really
the thinking behind that.

39:52.246 --> 39:53.196 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

39:53.976 --> 39:56.846 align:middle
Do you think the Fed has
a credibility problem?

39:57.316 --> 39:57.776 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:58.136 --> 39:58.926 align:middle
No, I don't.

39:59.266 --> 40:05.986 align:middle
And a good example of why
would be-in the fourth quarter

40:05.986 --> 40:10.186 align:middle
of last year, as we started
talking about tapering sooner

40:10.186 --> 40:11.836 align:middle
and then raising
rates this year,

40:12.266 --> 40:14.156 align:middle
you saw financial
markets reacting,

40:14.946 --> 40:16.216 align:middle
you know, very appropriately.

40:16.216 --> 40:18.576 align:middle
Not to bless any
particular day's measure,

40:18.576 --> 40:21.556 align:middle
but the way financial markets,

40:22.096 --> 40:25.156 align:middle
the forward rate curve
has tightened in response

40:25.156 --> 40:30.796 align:middle
to our guidance and our actions
really amplifies our policy.

40:31.166 --> 40:36.556 align:middle
It's-monetary policy is working
through expectations now,

40:36.606 --> 40:37.706 align:middle
to a very large extent.

40:37.706 --> 40:39.236 align:middle
We've only done two
rate increases.

40:39.306 --> 40:41.686 align:middle
But if you look at
financial conditions,

40:41.746 --> 40:44.646 align:middle
the two-year [rate] is at
280 [basis points] now.

40:44.646 --> 40:46.766 align:middle
In September, I think it
was at 20 basis points.

40:47.256 --> 40:48.716 align:middle
And that's all through
the economy.

40:48.716 --> 40:50.956 align:middle
People are feeling those
higher rates already.

40:51.826 --> 40:55.116 align:middle
And so that shows
that the markets think

40:55.116 --> 40:56.926 align:middle
that our forward
guidance is credible.

40:57.526 --> 41:00.846 align:middle
And I think we want
to keep it that way.

41:02.286 --> 41:03.296 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:03.456 --> 41:04.356 align:middle
Scott Horsley.

41:05.086 --> 41:05.336 align:middle
[No response] Okay.

41:05.866 --> 41:06.336 align:middle
Brian Cheung.

41:06.596 --> 41:08.486 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

41:09.246 --> 41:13.066 align:middle
Hi there. Brian Cheung
with Yahoo Finance.

41:13.066 --> 41:15.606 align:middle
To expand on Steve's
question about Paul Volcker,

41:15.606 --> 41:17.666 align:middle
there was also a great
pain that came with that

41:17.666 --> 41:19.786 align:middle
as well-higher interest rates,

41:19.786 --> 41:22.056 align:middle
obviously affecting
households and businesses.

41:22.056 --> 41:23.226 align:middle
I'm wondering how you kind

41:23.616 --> 41:25.946 align:middle
of square what might
be demand disruption.

41:25.946 --> 41:27.356 align:middle
Are you already seeing that?

41:27.356 --> 41:30.976 align:middle
Is the idea here to
incentivize a lack of spending,

41:30.976 --> 41:32.346 align:middle
to decrease consumption,

41:32.346 --> 41:34.636 align:middle
to perhaps table
business investments?

41:34.776 --> 41:36.046 align:middle
Is that essentially
what's happening

41:36.046 --> 41:36.826 align:middle
through this hiking cycle?

41:37.156 --> 41:37.666 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

41:37.666 --> 41:40.996 align:middle
Well, so, as I mentioned,
you can see places

41:40.996 --> 41:43.536 align:middle
where the demand
is substantially

41:43.536 --> 41:44.586 align:middle
in excess of supply.

41:44.586 --> 41:47.456 align:middle
And what you're seeing as a
result of that is prices going

41:47.876 --> 41:50.696 align:middle
up and at unsustainable levels,
levels that are not consistent

41:50.696 --> 41:51.706 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation.

41:52.356 --> 41:56.346 align:middle
And so what our tools do is
that as we raise interest rates,

41:56.836 --> 42:00.166 align:middle
demand moderates: it moves down.

42:00.166 --> 42:00.836 align:middle
Interest rates,

42:01.086 --> 42:03.616 align:middle
you know-businesses will
invest a little bit less,

42:03.616 --> 42:05.586 align:middle
consumers will spend
a little bit less.

42:05.996 --> 42:06.786 align:middle
That's how it works.

42:07.186 --> 42:11.106 align:middle
But, ultimately, getting those,
getting supply and demand back,

42:11.106 --> 42:12.336 align:middle
you know, back in balance,

42:12.716 --> 42:14.526 align:middle
is what gives us 2
percent inflation,

42:14.526 --> 42:17.476 align:middle
which is what gives
the economy a footing

42:17.606 --> 42:20.976 align:middle
where people can lead
successful economic lives

42:20.976 --> 42:22.086 align:middle
and not worry about inflation.

42:23.036 --> 42:25.646 align:middle
I mean, so, yes, there may
be some pain associated

42:25.646 --> 42:26.556 align:middle
with getting back to that.

42:26.586 --> 42:30.036 align:middle
But, you know, the big pain
over time is in not dealing

42:30.036 --> 42:32.516 align:middle
with inflation and allowing
it to become entrenched.

42:33.036 --> 42:34.156 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:34.616 --> 42:35.366 align:middle
Greg Robb.

42:37.156 --> 42:38.656 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

42:38.656 --> 42:38.976 align:middle
Thank you.

42:39.086 --> 42:40.066 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

42:40.106 --> 42:41.386 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

42:42.456 --> 42:45.906 align:middle
I was wondering if you could
take a step back and talk about,

42:46.036 --> 42:50.476 align:middle
in March the dot
plot had, you know,

42:50.476 --> 42:54.206 align:middle
looked like steady quarter-point
rate hikes, get the funds rate

42:54.206 --> 42:55.586 align:middle
up to 2 percent at
the end of the year.

42:56.476 --> 43:00.196 align:middle
Now-where it seems like
you're much more aggressive.

43:00.196 --> 43:02.936 align:middle
So could you talk about the
thinking that's behind that?

43:03.346 --> 43:03.586 align:middle
Thank you.

43:04.056 --> 43:04.396 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:04.646 --> 43:10.896 align:middle
So, look, I think what you've
seen is-really, I would say,

43:10.896 --> 43:15.996 align:middle
in the middle of last
fall-there was a time

43:15.996 --> 43:20.516 align:middle
when our policy stance was
still pretty much in sync

43:20.516 --> 43:21.616 align:middle
with what the data were saying.

43:21.616 --> 43:24.736 align:middle
If you remember, there were a
couple of weak jobs reports.

43:25.276 --> 43:28.406 align:middle
And inflation-month by
month, inflation had come

43:28.406 --> 43:32.796 align:middle
down till September, a few
months in a row, stayed low.

43:33.106 --> 43:37.236 align:middle
And then around the end
of October, we got three

43:37.416 --> 43:41.486 align:middle
or four really strong
readings that just said, no,

43:41.656 --> 43:43.206 align:middle
this is a much stronger economy.

43:43.686 --> 43:47.726 align:middle
And by the way-then, with the
restatement of the jobs numbers,

43:47.726 --> 43:50.916 align:middle
it looked like the job market
was much more even and stronger

43:50.916 --> 43:51.896 align:middle
in the second half of the year.

43:51.896 --> 43:53.496 align:middle
But that hadn't happened yet.

43:53.846 --> 43:57.326 align:middle
Anyway, we got an ECI
reading-employment compensation,

43:57.326 --> 43:58.726 align:middle
[employment cost] index
reading-the Friday before the

43:58.726 --> 43:59.436 align:middle
November meeting.

43:59.436 --> 44:01.566 align:middle
Then we got a really
strong jobs report.

44:01.566 --> 44:03.826 align:middle
Then we got a really
high CPI report.

44:04.316 --> 44:06.166 align:middle
And so I think it became
clear to the Committee

44:06.166 --> 44:09.076 align:middle
that we needed to
adjust and adapt.

44:09.236 --> 44:09.876 align:middle
And we have.

44:09.876 --> 44:12.956 align:middle
Ever since then, really ever
since then, we've been adapting.

44:13.486 --> 44:17.416 align:middle
We, you know-the
Committee moved by the time

44:17.416 --> 44:18.376 align:middle
of the December meeting

44:18.376 --> 44:18.966 align:middle
to a median [in participants'
2022 projections]

44:18.966 --> 44:20.736 align:middle
of three rate increases,
then to a median

44:20.736 --> 44:22.496 align:middle
of seven increases
at the March meeting.

44:22.736 --> 44:24.326 align:middle
And that process is going on.

44:24.326 --> 44:25.826 align:middle
And it's clearly continuing.

44:25.826 --> 44:28.386 align:middle
And that's why I say-and
I actually mentioned this

44:28.656 --> 44:32.796 align:middle
at the March meeting-that no one
should look at any single SEP

44:33.596 --> 44:36.836 align:middle
as sort of a real resting place
for 90 days, because we're

44:36.836 --> 44:38.396 align:middle
in a fast-evolving situation.

44:38.396 --> 44:39.196 align:middle
And that's what's happened.

44:39.536 --> 44:42.776 align:middle
You can see-unanimous
vote today, of course.

44:42.856 --> 44:46.846 align:middle
And I told you the guidance-that
broad support on the Committee

44:47.666 --> 44:50.476 align:middle
to have 50-basis-point
hikes on the table

44:50.616 --> 44:52.356 align:middle
at the next couple of meetings.

44:52.356 --> 44:53.836 align:middle
So you're right.

44:54.106 --> 44:54.866 align:middle
And by the way,

44:54.866 --> 44:57.426 align:middle
other forecasters have
been doing the same thing.

44:57.996 --> 45:01.286 align:middle
And it's just us adapting to
the data and to the situation

45:01.336 --> 45:03.146 align:middle
and using our tools
to deal with it.

45:03.146 --> 45:03.546 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

45:04.946 --> 45:06.776 align:middle
Thanks. We'll go to Nancy
for the last question.

45:06.776 --> 45:08.596 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

45:08.796 --> 45:11.456 align:middle
Hi. Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

45:11.456 --> 45:14.076 align:middle
Chair Powell, I want
to ask how you're able

45:14.186 --> 45:17.176 align:middle
to balance your dual
mandate-stable prices

45:17.176 --> 45:20.166 align:middle
and maximum
employment-especially

45:20.516 --> 45:22.036 align:middle
when the unemployment rate

45:22.086 --> 45:24.996 align:middle
for Black workers is
still roughly double,

45:25.026 --> 45:27.706 align:middle
roughly twice the rate
for white workers.

45:28.316 --> 45:28.776 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:28.996 --> 45:33.116 align:middle
So unemployment rates for
all racial groups have come

45:33.116 --> 45:35.846 align:middle
down a lot and are
now much closer

45:35.846 --> 45:38.566 align:middle
to where they were
before the pandemic hit.

45:38.566 --> 45:42.726 align:middle
So that's one thing I would say.

45:42.726 --> 45:44.826 align:middle
And that's important.

45:45.346 --> 45:48.916 align:middle
But the bigger point is
this: I do not, at this time,

45:48.916 --> 45:52.686 align:middle
see the two sides of the
mandate as in tension.

45:52.816 --> 45:54.846 align:middle
I don't, because you can see

45:54.846 --> 45:56.596 align:middle
that the labor market
is out of balance.

45:56.716 --> 45:59.346 align:middle
You can see that
there's a labor shortage.

45:59.636 --> 46:02.226 align:middle
There aren't enough people
to fill these job openings.

46:02.906 --> 46:09.536 align:middle
And companies can't hire, and
wages are moving up at levels

46:09.536 --> 46:11.446 align:middle
that would not, over
time, be consistent

46:11.446 --> 46:13.506 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation
over time.

46:14.346 --> 46:16.266 align:middle
And, of course, everyone
loves to see wages go up.

46:16.266 --> 46:17.716 align:middle
And it's a great thing,
but you want them to go

46:17.716 --> 46:20.576 align:middle
up at a sustainable level,
because these wages are,

46:20.746 --> 46:22.766 align:middle
to some extent, being
eaten up by inflation.

46:23.366 --> 46:26.436 align:middle
So, what that really
means is, to get the kind

46:26.436 --> 46:29.016 align:middle
of labor market we really
want to get, we really want

46:29.016 --> 46:31.346 align:middle
to have a labor market
that serves all Americans,

46:31.766 --> 46:35.536 align:middle
especially the people
in the lower-income part

46:35.536 --> 46:37.746 align:middle
of the distribution,
especially them.

46:37.926 --> 46:39.566 align:middle
To do that, you've got
to have price stability.

46:39.996 --> 46:41.656 align:middle
And we've got to get
back to price stability

46:41.656 --> 46:43.656 align:middle
so that we can have
a labor market

46:43.656 --> 46:47.156 align:middle
where people's wages aren't
being eaten up by inflation

46:47.156 --> 46:49.746 align:middle
and where we can have
a long expansion, too.

46:49.746 --> 46:51.486 align:middle
That's-the good things
you can have, as we have.

46:51.486 --> 46:53.966 align:middle
We've had-several of
the longest expansions

46:53.966 --> 46:56.736 align:middle
in U.S. history have been
in the last 40 years,

46:56.906 --> 46:59.136 align:middle
and that's because it's been
a time of low inflation.

46:59.136 --> 47:02.756 align:middle
And long expansions
are good for people

47:02.986 --> 47:04.106 align:middle
and good for the labor market.

47:04.156 --> 47:06.426 align:middle
So that's the way
I think about it.

47:06.426 --> 47:07.936 align:middle
I think we-you know,
our tools work.

47:07.936 --> 47:10.206 align:middle
We have to think in the, in
the medium and longer term.

47:10.816 --> 47:14.826 align:middle
And I do think that the best
thing for everyone is for us

47:14.826 --> 47:18.176 align:middle
to get back to price
stability to support, really,

47:18.256 --> 47:21.246 align:middle
a sustained period of strong
labor market conditions.

47:22.116 --> 47:25.996 align:middle
Thanks very much.

